WEBVTT

00:00.350 --> 00:03.521
On October eleventh, twenty
twenty-three, a ticket

00:03.561 --> 00:06.411
sold in California won
the Powerball jackpot:

00:06.571 --> 00:09.070
one point seven six five billion dollars.

00:09.430 --> 00:10.777
But the winner had a choice.

00:11.137 --> 00:13.806
Either thirty payments spread
over twenty-nine years.

00:14.166 --> 00:18.670
Or seven hundred and seventy-four million
dollars right away, less than half.

00:19.030 --> 00:20.078
Who loses out?

00:20.718 --> 00:23.451
By the end of this video,
you'll be able to work it out.

00:24.341 --> 00:26.797
Hi, and welcome to the Actuarial path!

00:27.237 --> 00:28.456
Thanks for being here.

00:28.816 --> 00:32.637
Today we build the actuary's
financial toolkit, step by step.

00:32.997 --> 00:34.813
No finance background needed:

00:34.973 --> 00:38.026
if you can compute a percentage
and a power, you're set.

00:38.386 --> 00:40.138
We'll see everything else together.

00:41.028 --> 00:42.055
Here's the plan.

00:42.415 --> 00:44.273
Simple and compound interest.

00:44.633 --> 00:46.300
Discounting and the factor v.

00:46.660 --> 00:47.857
The rate of discount.

00:48.217 --> 00:49.351
Equivalent rates.

00:49.711 --> 00:50.801
Annuities certain.

00:51.161 --> 00:53.255
And to finish, a worked exercise.

00:54.145 --> 00:55.961
Let's start with the price of time:

00:56.121 --> 00:57.233
the interest rate.

00:58.123 --> 01:00.600
When you lend money, you
go without it for a while:

01:00.760 --> 01:02.811
you can't spend it or use it elsewhere.

01:03.171 --> 01:05.158
The borrower pays you rent for that time:

01:05.318 --> 01:06.323
that's interest.

01:06.683 --> 01:09.477
The interest rate is that
rent as a percentage of

01:09.517 --> 01:12.254
the amount lent, for one
period, usually a year.

01:13.144 --> 01:15.707
For an insurer, this is
the heart of the business.

01:16.067 --> 01:19.834
JG's Holding, our fictional
insurer, collects its customers'

01:19.874 --> 01:23.515
premiums today, and will only
pay benefits years from now.

01:23.875 --> 01:26.907
In between, the money is
invested and earns interest.

01:27.267 --> 01:31.536
Throughout this video, we'll use a made-up
rate of two point five percent a year.

01:32.426 --> 01:33.709
First way of counting:

01:33.869 --> 01:34.981
simple interest.

01:35.341 --> 01:38.181
Interest is always computed
on the starting amount.

01:38.541 --> 01:43.471
One thousand dollars at two point five
percent earns twenty-five dollars every year.

01:43.831 --> 01:47.290
After ten years, one thousand
plus ten times twenty-five:

01:47.450 --> 01:49.842
one thousand two hundred
and fifty dollars.

01:50.202 --> 01:53.597
The interest already earned sits
to one side, and earns nothing.

01:54.487 --> 01:55.535
As a formula:

01:55.695 --> 02:00.178
the future value equals the starting
amount times one plus i times n.

02:00.538 --> 02:03.058
Each year adds the same
twenty-five dollar step:

02:03.218 --> 02:05.055
the balance grows in a straight line.

02:05.415 --> 02:06.996
And n needn't be a whole number:

02:07.156 --> 02:09.100
for six months, n is one half.

02:09.990 --> 02:14.771
Simple interest really exists, on France's
most widely held savings account:

02:14.931 --> 02:15.873
the Livret A.

02:16.233 --> 02:19.085
Since August first, twenty
twenty-six, its rate

02:19.125 --> 02:21.547
has been one point seven percent a year.

02:21.907 --> 02:25.067
During the year, interest is
counted by whole half-months:

02:25.227 --> 02:26.873
a year has twenty-four of them.

02:27.233 --> 02:31.481
One thousand euros paid in on August
tenth starts earning on the sixteenth:

02:31.641 --> 02:34.737
that leaves nine half-months
until December thirty-first.

02:35.097 --> 02:35.910
Interest:

02:36.070 --> 02:40.373
one thousand times one point seven
percent, times nine twenty-fourths,

02:40.413 --> 02:42.793
about six euros and thirty-eight cents.

02:43.153 --> 02:47.657
That's exactly simple interest, with n
equal to nine twenty-fourths of a year.

02:48.547 --> 02:50.342
What if interest earned interest too?

02:51.232 --> 02:52.771
Let's stay with the Livret A.

02:53.131 --> 02:56.973
On December thirty-first, the year's
interest is added to the balance.

02:57.333 --> 03:00.917
So the following year, one thousand
and six euros thirty-eight

03:00.957 --> 03:03.139
earns interest, not just one thousand.

03:03.499 --> 03:05.976
Last year's interest
earns interest in turn:

03:06.136 --> 03:07.611
that's compound interest.

03:07.971 --> 03:10.256
We also say the interest is capitalized.

03:11.146 --> 03:14.022
Back to our one thousand
dollars at two point five

03:14.062 --> 03:16.354
percent, with interest added every year.

03:16.714 --> 03:21.282
After one year, one thousand and twenty-five
dollars, just like simple interest.

03:21.642 --> 03:24.475
But in the second year,
the two point five percent

03:24.515 --> 03:27.234
applies to one thousand
and twenty-five dollars:

03:27.394 --> 03:30.818
we get one thousand and fifty
dollars and sixty-three cents,

03:30.858 --> 03:33.647
sixty-three cents more
than with simple interest.

03:34.007 --> 03:38.042
After ten years, one thousand two hundred
and eighty dollars and eight cents:

03:38.202 --> 03:39.442
thirty dollars more.

03:40.332 --> 03:41.273
Why a power?

03:41.913 --> 03:45.180
Each year, the amount is
multiplied by one plus i.

03:45.540 --> 03:47.911
After two years, we've multiplied twice;

03:48.151 --> 03:49.775
after n years, n times.

03:50.135 --> 03:51.289
Hence the formula:

03:51.449 --> 03:56.103
the future value equals the starting
amount times one plus i, to the power n.

03:56.463 --> 04:00.156
This one plus i to the n is
called the accumulation factor.

04:00.516 --> 04:04.721
And i here is the annual effective rate,
the rate actually earned in a year.

04:05.611 --> 04:09.390
Look at both ways of counting, year
after year, over thirty years.

04:09.750 --> 04:11.587
At the bottom, the starting amount:

04:11.747 --> 04:13.798
one thousand dollars, always there.

04:14.158 --> 04:16.102
Above it, the interest on the capital:

04:16.262 --> 04:20.257
twenty-five dollars a year, the same
step every year, so one thousand

04:20.297 --> 04:23.240
seven hundred and fifty
dollars after thirty years.

04:23.600 --> 04:26.288
And in gold, the interest
on interest, almost

04:26.328 --> 04:28.894
invisible at first,
then bigger and bigger:

04:29.054 --> 04:33.064
after thirty years, we reach two
thousand and ninety-seven dollars and

04:33.104 --> 04:36.651
fifty-seven cents, three hundred
and forty-seven dollars more.

04:37.011 --> 04:39.574
That's the snowball effect,
and it grows with time.

04:40.464 --> 04:41.853
Remember the rule of thumb.

04:42.213 --> 04:46.461
Under a year, as on the Livret A during
the year, interest is often simple.

04:46.821 --> 04:48.851
Over several years, it's compound.

04:49.211 --> 04:53.151
And actuaries, who reason over
thirty- or forty-year contracts,

04:53.191 --> 04:55.805
almost always work with compound interest.

04:56.165 --> 04:57.704
That's what we'll do from now on.

04:58.594 --> 05:01.157
Actuaries mostly need
the reverse question.

05:02.047 --> 05:06.060
JG's Holding has promised to pay
one thousand dollars in ten years.

05:06.420 --> 05:09.076
How much should it set
aside today, invested at

05:09.116 --> 05:11.714
two point five percent,
to be sure it can pay?

05:12.354 --> 05:14.596
We no longer go from
today into the future:

05:14.756 --> 05:17.340
we start from the future
and come back to today.

05:17.700 --> 05:18.855
That's discounting.

05:19.745 --> 05:21.817
To go back in time, we do the reverse:

05:21.977 --> 05:24.924
divide by one plus i
instead of multiplying.

05:25.284 --> 05:29.318
Actuaries write v for this factor,
one divided by one plus i:

05:29.478 --> 05:33.748
at two point five percent, about
zero point nine seven five six one.

05:34.108 --> 05:38.612
So one dollar payable in a year is worth
about ninety-seven and a half cents today.

05:38.972 --> 05:42.174
Over n years, we multiply
by v to the power n.

05:42.534 --> 05:44.841
Here, one thousand times v to the ten:

05:45.001 --> 05:48.033
seven hundred and eighty-one
dollars and twenty cents.

05:48.923 --> 05:50.440
Let's see it on a timeline.

05:50.800 --> 05:53.086
The promised payment
is here, in ten years:

05:53.246 --> 05:54.550
one thousand dollars.

05:54.910 --> 05:59.691
To bring it back to today, we multiply it
by v for each year we cross, ten times:

05:59.851 --> 06:00.750
it shrinks.

06:01.110 --> 06:03.617
When it reaches today,
it's worth seven hundred

06:03.657 --> 06:05.784
and eighty-one dollars and twenty cents.

06:06.674 --> 06:08.021
Let's check the other way:

06:08.181 --> 06:12.465
seven hundred and eighty-one dollars
and twenty cents, invested for ten

06:12.505 --> 06:16.546
years at two point five percent,
does grow to one thousand dollars.

06:16.906 --> 06:20.983
Accumulating moves forward in
time, multiplying by one plus i.

06:21.343 --> 06:24.055
Discounting moves back, multiplying by v.

06:24.415 --> 06:26.829
All of insurance rests
on this back and forth.

06:27.719 --> 06:29.812
The same deal can be told in two ways.

06:31.386 --> 06:32.775
A real fact to begin.

06:33.135 --> 06:35.872
The US Treasury borrows
short term with bills

06:35.912 --> 06:38.279
that pay no interest during their life.

06:38.639 --> 06:40.413
So how does the lender earn anything?

06:41.053 --> 06:43.496
They buy the bill for
less than its face value,

06:43.536 --> 06:45.557
and receive the face value at maturity.

06:45.917 --> 06:48.957
The interest is held back from
the start, and the Treasury

06:48.997 --> 06:51.082
quotes these bills with a discount rate.

06:51.442 --> 06:54.858
The same mechanism exists when
a bank discounts an invoice.

06:55.748 --> 06:57.052
Let's take an example.

06:57.412 --> 07:02.129
A company holds a one thousand dollar invoice
that its customer will pay in a year.

07:02.489 --> 07:03.815
It wants the money now:

07:03.975 --> 07:07.894
the bank pays it nine hundred and
seventy-five dollars and sixty-one

07:07.934 --> 07:11.679
cents today, and keeps twenty-four
dollars and thirty-nine cents.

07:12.039 --> 07:14.495
That amount held back can
be measured two ways:

07:14.655 --> 07:18.022
relative to the one thousand
dollars at the end, it's the

07:18.062 --> 07:21.249
rate of discount d, about
two point four four percent;

07:21.489 --> 07:25.120
relative to the nine hundred
and seventy-five dollars lent,

07:25.160 --> 07:28.169
it's the interest rate i,
two point five percent.

07:29.059 --> 07:34.139
As a formula, d equals i divided
by one plus i, that is i times v:

07:34.299 --> 07:37.395
the same interest, but paid a
year earlier, so discounted.

07:37.755 --> 07:40.040
You can also write it as one minus v:

07:40.200 --> 07:44.598
for one dollar due in a year, you get v
today, and the discount is what's missing.

07:44.958 --> 07:48.651
Since v is less than one, d is
always a little smaller than i.

07:49.011 --> 07:52.043
The rate of discount will be
back very soon, in annuities.

07:52.933 --> 07:55.752
The same investment can be
quoted with different rates.

07:56.642 --> 07:57.818
Another real fact.

07:58.178 --> 08:02.545
In France, every lender is legally
required to show the TAEG, the annual

08:02.585 --> 08:06.095
percentage rate, in its ads,
offers and credit agreements.

08:06.455 --> 08:10.231
It expresses the total cost of
the loan as a yearly percentage,

08:10.271 --> 08:13.199
so that offers can be
compared on the same basis.

08:13.559 --> 08:14.821
Why the same basis?

08:15.461 --> 08:19.041
Because a loan repaid monthly can
be quoted with a monthly rate

08:19.081 --> 08:22.546
or a yearly rate, and those rates
can't be compared directly.

08:23.436 --> 08:25.913
Let's compare two ways of
reaching the same result.

08:26.273 --> 08:29.391
One thousand dollars at two
point five percent a year:

08:29.551 --> 08:34.140
a single jump, at the end of the year, up
to one thousand and twenty-five dollars.

08:34.500 --> 08:39.324
Or twelve small jumps, one a month, at
zero point two zero six percent each:

08:39.484 --> 08:43.311
since each month counts the interest
of the months before, we land

08:43.351 --> 08:47.060
in exactly the same place, one
thousand and twenty-five dollars.

08:47.420 --> 08:49.065
These two rates are equivalent.

08:49.425 --> 08:50.580
But beware the trap:

08:50.740 --> 08:54.518
twelve times zero point two
zero six percent is only two

08:54.558 --> 08:57.996
point four seven percent,
less than two point five.

08:58.886 --> 08:59.934
As a formula:

09:00.094 --> 09:03.806
a monthly rate j is equivalent
to the annual rate i if

09:03.846 --> 09:07.350
one plus j, to the power
twelve, equals one plus i.

09:07.710 --> 09:08.715
That gives j:

09:08.875 --> 09:12.590
one point zero two five to the
power one twelfth, minus one.

09:12.950 --> 09:16.526
And twelve times j is the
nominal rate, written i with a

09:16.566 --> 09:20.078
twelve in brackets, about two
point four seven percent.

09:20.438 --> 09:23.747
A nominal rate is just a way
of quoting the monthly rate.

09:24.107 --> 09:27.865
To compare two offers, always go
back to the annual effective rate.

09:28.755 --> 09:32.427
And what if we compounded every
day, every hour, every second?

09:33.067 --> 09:35.996
In the limit, we compound
continuously, and the rate

09:36.036 --> 09:38.680
is called the force of
interest, written delta.

09:39.040 --> 09:44.483
One plus i equals e to the delta, so
delta is the natural log of one plus i:

09:44.643 --> 09:46.630
about two point four six nine percent.

09:46.990 --> 09:49.424
All these rates describe
the same investment.

09:49.784 --> 09:53.721
The smallest is the rate of discount,
then delta, then the monthly

09:53.761 --> 09:57.275
nominal rate, and the largest
is the annual effective rate.

09:58.165 --> 10:00.216
Now let's move on to several payments.

10:01.106 --> 10:03.733
An annuity is a series
of regular payments:

10:03.893 --> 10:05.879
rent, loan repayments, a pension.

10:06.239 --> 10:08.687
It's called certain when
every payment will be

10:08.727 --> 10:10.850
made no matter what, with no conditions.

10:11.210 --> 10:13.965
To find its value today,
there's only one method:

10:14.125 --> 10:17.050
discount each payment from
its date, then add them up.

10:17.940 --> 10:20.565
JG's Holding must pay
one thousand dollars at

10:20.605 --> 10:22.935
the end of each of the next three years.

10:23.295 --> 10:24.812
Here they are on the timeline.

10:25.172 --> 10:26.860
We bring each one back to today:

10:27.020 --> 10:31.151
the first is worth nine hundred and
seventy-five dollars and sixty-one

10:31.191 --> 10:35.561
cents, the second nine hundred and
fifty-one dollars and eighty-one cents,

10:35.601 --> 10:39.375
the third nine hundred and
twenty-eight dollars and sixty cents.

10:39.735 --> 10:42.424
Stacked up, they're worth
two thousand eight hundred

10:42.464 --> 10:44.943
and fifty-six dollars
today, not three thousand.

10:45.833 --> 10:48.596
For a payment of one at
the end of each year, for

10:48.636 --> 10:50.998
n years, the present value is written a n:

10:51.158 --> 10:52.505
the annuity immediate.

10:52.865 --> 10:56.238
It's v, plus v squared, and
so on up to v to the n.

10:56.598 --> 10:59.588
This geometric sum boils
down to one formula:

10:59.748 --> 11:02.438
one minus v to the n, divided by i.

11:02.798 --> 11:05.553
Over three years, about
two point eight five six:

11:05.713 --> 11:08.532
our two thousand eight hundred
and fifty-six dollars.

11:08.892 --> 11:11.476
Over ten years, about
eight point seven five:

11:11.636 --> 11:15.265
ten payments of one are worth only
eight point seven five today.

11:16.155 --> 11:20.446
And what if each payment fell at the start
of the year, like rent paid in advance?

11:21.086 --> 11:22.731
The first payment falls today:

11:22.891 --> 11:25.859
it's worth one thousand
dollars, with no discounting.

11:26.219 --> 11:29.528
The other two come a year earlier
than before, so they shrink less.

11:29.888 --> 11:34.563
In total, two thousand nine hundred and
twenty-seven dollars and forty-two cents.

11:35.453 --> 11:38.355
This annuity paid at the
start of each year is called

11:38.395 --> 11:40.576
the annuity due, written a double-dot n.

11:40.936 --> 11:44.005
Each payment comes a year
earlier than in the annuity

11:44.045 --> 11:46.997
immediate, so we multiply
everything by one plus i.

11:47.357 --> 11:49.280
And the rate of discount shows up again:

11:49.440 --> 11:53.517
a double-dot n equals one minus
v to the n, divided by d.

11:53.877 --> 11:54.776
Makes sense:

11:54.936 --> 11:58.608
the interest on a payment at the
start of the year is paid in advance.

11:59.498 --> 12:00.994
Back to our Powerball winner.

12:01.354 --> 12:04.809
The advertised one point seven
six five billion dollars

12:04.849 --> 12:07.351
is the plain sum of the thirty payments:

12:07.511 --> 12:12.335
one right away, then twenty-nine annual
payments that grow by five percent a year.

12:12.695 --> 12:17.157
But a dollar received in twenty-nine years
is worth far less than a dollar today.

12:17.517 --> 12:21.205
The seven hundred and seventy-four
million offered right away,

12:21.245 --> 12:24.693
about forty-four percent of
the advertised amount, roughly

12:24.733 --> 12:27.759
matches the present value
of those thirty payments.

12:28.119 --> 12:29.381
Nobody loses out:

12:29.541 --> 12:32.829
they're two ways of presenting the
same wealth, at different dates.

12:33.719 --> 12:35.322
Keep this building block safe.

12:35.682 --> 12:40.268
In the life and mortality module, a
life annuity will be an annuity where

12:40.308 --> 12:44.260
each payment is made only if the
insured person is still alive.

12:44.620 --> 12:47.558
We'll discount just as we
did today, but also take

12:47.598 --> 12:49.999
the probability of survival into account.

12:50.359 --> 12:52.858
The rest of the path builds
on what you've just learned.

12:53.748 --> 12:54.583
Your turn.

12:56.248 --> 12:57.296
Pause the video.

12:57.656 --> 13:01.953
With an annual effective rate of four
percent, over three years, compute

13:01.993 --> 13:06.411
the rate of discount d, the force of
interest delta, the annuity immediate

13:06.451 --> 13:09.243
a three and the annuity
due a double-dot three.

13:30.633 --> 13:31.446
Solution.

13:31.806 --> 13:33.004
The rate of discount:

13:33.164 --> 13:36.456
zero point zero four
divided by one point zero

13:36.496 --> 13:39.716
four, about zero point
zero three eight five.

13:40.076 --> 13:41.380
The force of interest:

13:41.540 --> 13:46.620
the natural log of one point zero four,
about zero point zero three nine two.

13:46.980 --> 13:48.348
The annuity immediate:

13:48.508 --> 13:52.727
one minus one point zero four
to the minus three, divided by

13:52.767 --> 13:56.916
zero point zero four, about two
point seven seven five one.

13:57.276 --> 13:58.473
And the annuity due:

13:58.633 --> 14:02.817
one point zero four times a three,
about two point eight eight six one.

14:03.177 --> 14:06.700
Once again, d is smaller than
delta, which is smaller than i.

14:07.590 --> 14:08.851
Here's what to remember.

14:09.211 --> 14:12.414
With simple interest,
multiply by one plus i n;

14:12.654 --> 14:15.515
with compound interest,
by one plus i to the n.

14:15.875 --> 14:17.393
Discount with v to the n.

14:17.753 --> 14:20.422
The rate of discount is
interest paid in advance:

14:20.582 --> 14:22.249
d equals i times v.

14:22.609 --> 14:26.174
Rates are equivalent if they give
the same amount after one year.

14:26.534 --> 14:29.289
And an annuity is a sum
of discounted payments:

14:29.449 --> 14:34.977
one minus v to the n over i at year end,
times one plus i at the start of the year.

14:35.867 --> 14:37.235
Your toolkit is ready.

14:37.595 --> 14:38.686
Next episode:

14:38.846 --> 14:43.243
we add mortality, to work out what a
promise to pay on death is worth today.

14:43.603 --> 14:48.043
If this video helped, subscribe, leave a
like and share it with someone it'll help.

14:48.403 --> 14:51.904
And if the exercise gives you
trouble, write to us in the comments:

14:52.064 --> 14:52.856
we'll help.

14:53.216 --> 14:55.224
Thanks for watching, and see you soon!

14:56.114 --> 14:57.141
ACTU-BASICS.

14:57.501 --> 14:58.954
Enlightening through numbers.
